# Scaladoc for companion objects

A **companion** is an `object` with the same name as a `class`, `case class`, or `trait` in the same file (e.g. `case class QueryLayerConfig` and `object QueryLayerConfig`).

This is **not** the same as a standalone object such as `SchemaUtils`, which has no paired type and **does** get class-level Scaladoc (see `scaladoc-classes`).

## What to document

- **Do not** write class-level (object-level) Scaladoc on a companion. The paired type already carries that description.
- **Do** document **every** method declared in the companion, using `scaladoc-methods` (summary, `@param`, `@tparam`, `@return`, `@throws`, `@since`). Method Scaladoc is never skipped because the method lives on a companion.

This applies to production companions and to test-suite companions (`object FooSpec`).

## Do / don't

```scala
// ❌ BAD — class-level Scaladoc on a companion
/**
 * Property keys used by QueryLayerConfig.
 *
 * @since 0.1.0
 */
object QueryLayerConfig {
  def fromMap(properties: util.Map[String, String]): QueryLayerConfig = ...
}

// ✅ GOOD — no class-level Scaladoc; methods always documented
object QueryLayerConfig {

  /**
   * Builds a query-layer configuration from a property map.
   *
   * @param properties configuration entries keyed by property name
   * @return a query-layer configuration
   * @since 0.1.0
   */
  def fromMap(properties: util.Map[String, String]): QueryLayerConfig = ...
}
```

## Notes

- Do not add class-level Scaladoc to a companion; if one is already there, leave it alone unless the user asks to remove it.
- Empty `TODO` class-level comments on companions should be removed, not filled in.
- Standalone objects (no paired type) are documented at class level; see `scaladoc-classes`.
